The `rework-on-validation-failed` rule (line ~192): queries label validation_failed + checks red, localAction arm_rework. Benching dead-head-parked candidates in queryPrs would also stop this rule from arming rework on a dead-head parked machine PR — the reviewer's "related dead end" — because the candidate never survives the query. 

But wait: is that desirable? The reviewer says "Consider exempting dead-head parks from the machine rework arm." Benching at source = the rule never sees the candidate = no arm. And the park RESET probes: park-reset rule (line ~249) routes parked non-validating PRs through update_branch's RESET probe — update_branch is a localAction with cli clone/push on... the head branch. For a dead head, the clone of a deleted branch fails (fail-safe) — but it burns a clone per tick! Benching dead-head-parked candidates from park-reset too? Hmm — careful: "do NOT exclude all validation_failed — the park-reset rule must still find red-parked PRs". A dead-head park CAN be cleared: "Restore or re-push the branch — a new human push clears the park automatically". If the branch is restored, branchExists becomes true → not benched → park-reset runs. If branch is gone, park-reset would try update_branch → clone branch fails → harmless no-op but burns a runner clone each tick. Benching it while the branch is absent is CORRECT — nothing can happen until the branch exists again, and the moment it exists, benching stops. The RESET probe semantics ("human push newer than the park") still work once the branch is restored.

Edge: branchHeads page-1 miss → branchExists does the single-branch gh api probe (#639) → true → not benched. Fail-safe.

Now where to put the bench in queryPrs: BEFORE the mutex scan or after `matched`? Important: a benched parked PR should not act as a mutex HOLDER either (like 'blocked'). The mutex scan happens on `items` before `matched`. The parked item holds no mutex labels in the dead-head scenario (validation_failed isn't a mutex). The ai_validating mutex: a dead-head-parked PR... could it hold ai_validating? The zombie sweep re-dispatches on dead heads (dispatch fails, no-ref → park validation_failed; does it drop the arm?). Beyond scope — the reviewer's failure shape is about candidates, so benching at the `matched` stage (after guards, before tier sort) is the right spot. Simpler and it keeps the mutex logic untouched.

Implementation in queryPrs:

```js
    // gh-922 (dead-head park bench): a validation_failed-parked PR whose
    // head ref is GONE can never be dispatched against ...
    matched = benchDeadHeadParks(matched, provider);
```

Where `benchDeadHeadParks(list, provider)` is a module-level helper:
```js
function benchDeadHeadParks(list, provider) {
    var parked = list.filter(function (it) {
        return it.branch && it.labels.indexOf(PARKED_LABEL) !== -1;
    });
    if (!parked.length || !provider || typeof provider.branchExists !== 'function') return list;
    return list.filter(function (it) {
        if (!it.branch || it.labels.indexOf(PARKED_LABEL) === -1) return true;
        var exists = true;
        try { exists = provider.branchExists(it.branch); } catch (eBench) { exists = true; } // fail open
        if (exists === false) {
            console.log('   ⏳ benched dead-head park pr-' + it.prNumber + ' — head ref ' + it.branch + ' is gone (gh-922): restore/re-push the branch to re-enter the queue');
        }
        return exists !== false;
    });
}
```
